00:11:53.912 --> 00:12:00.886 align:start size:97% position:3%
<i> Like the USS Texas, Invincible
 has fire proof blast doors</i>

00:12:00.919 --> 00:12:07.059 align:start size:88% position:13%
<i> that stop fires spreading
 preventing the entire
 vessel from exploding.</i>

00:12:07.092 --> 00:12:08.794
<i> So what goes wrong?</i>

00:12:08.827 --> 00:12:12.531
(explosions)

00:12:14.800 --> 00:12:18.203 line:0%
<i> (music)</i>

00:12:18.236 --> 00:12:24.576 line:0%
<i> Draining the water of the North
 Sea is uncovering secrets about
 the Battle of Jutland.</i>

00:12:24.609 --> 00:12:30.149 align:start size:94% position:6%
<i> And helping solve a
 controversial question is it
 the Germans or the British</i>

00:12:30.182 --> 00:12:32.885
<i> who win the battle?</i>

00:12:32.918 --> 00:12:37.923
<i> The drained wreck of
 HMS Invincible reveals
 extraordinary damage.</i>

00:12:37.956 --> 00:12:44.797
<i> Can the shattered remains offer
 clues about how the explosion
 snaps the ship clean in half.</i>

00:12:44.830 --> 00:12:48.367 align:start size:97% position:3% line:0%
PROF. GROVE: This is a
tremendous shock and it
produces very heavy casualties.

00:12:48.400 --> 00:12:53.272 line:0%
A ship of this size going up is
going to kill a thousand people.

00:12:53.305 --> 00:12:58.844 align:start position:0%
<i> NARRATOR: There is one substanc
 explosive enough to blow up a
 warship a type of Gunpowder,</i>

00:12:58.877 --> 00:13:06.885
<i> known as Cordite and
 battleships like Invincible and
 the USS TEXAS are packed with
 tons of it.</i>

00:13:06.918 --> 00:13:10.856
<i> It's what allows them to
 fire their deadly payload.</i>

00:13:16.328 --> 00:13:21.967
JURENS: The whole purpose
of this enormous ship is
to take this projectile,

00:13:22.000 --> 00:13:27.807
which weighs half as much as a
Toyota, and push it out ten or
fifteen miles to hit an enemy.

00:13:32.944 --> 00:13:39.952
Some Husky Sailors would lift up
4 of these big propellant bags.

00:13:39.985 --> 00:13:42.421
Inside was Cordite.

00:13:42.454 --> 00:13:49.328
Central station fires the gun,
ignites the cordite, pushing
this shell out about four times

00:13:49.361 --> 00:13:52.998
the speed that an
airliner flies.

00:13:53.999 --> 00:14:01.406
About 25 seconds later and ten
miles away, this thing comes
down and we hope hits the enemy.

00:14:01.439 --> 00:14:04.943
(explosion)

00:14:04.976 --> 00:14:10.115 align:start position:0%
<i> NARRATOR: But the danger for al
 battleships is that if their ow
 cordite store is ignited</i>

00:14:10.148 --> 00:14:16.188
<i> by an enemy shell, the
 entire vessel explodes.</i>

00:14:16.221 --> 00:14:21.627 align:start position:0%
<i> So extraordinary safety feature
 to keep fires from spreading ar
 built into the design</i>

00:14:21.660 --> 00:14:25.197 align:start size:84% position:16%
<i> of battleships like the
 USS Texas.</i>

00:14:25.230 --> 00:14:30.502 align:start size:97% position:3%
<i> Cordite is stored in magazines
 deep inside the ship.</i>

00:14:30.535 --> 00:14:36.876 align:start size:94% position:6%
<i> The metal blast doors seal
 the magazines preventing fire
 spreading down from the guns
 above.</i>

00:14:38.644 --> 00:14:45.217
<i> And the highly flammable bags
 of cordite are passed up to the
 guns through secure hatches.</i>

00:14:46.284 --> 00:14:53.558 align:start position:0%
<i> So with all these elaborate
 safety precautions in place wha
 goes wrong on the Invincible?</i>

00:14:53.591 --> 00:14:58.097 align:start size:94% position:6%
<i> VINA deploys a submersible
 camera to search for evidence
 around the turrets</i>

00:15:06.104 --> 00:15:10.075
JELLICOE: My gosh, look at
that, we're right underneath.

00:15:10.108 --> 00:15:12.544
There's something here.

00:15:12.577 --> 00:15:18.216 align:start size:84% position:16%
It does look more like
a shell doesn't it?

00:15:18.249 --> 00:15:24.990
So just below the shell one
can see the top of a cordite
container and strands of

00:15:25.023 --> 00:15:27.259
Cordite.

00:15:27.292 --> 00:15:33.098 align:start position:0%
Because in these long stick like
strands all bundled together.

00:15:33.131 --> 00:15:39.137
Quick burning, very unstable.

00:15:39.170 --> 00:15:43.108 align:start size:88% position:13%
<i> NARRATOR: The cordite
 and shells piled together
 in the wrecked turret,</i>

00:15:43.141 --> 00:15:46.445
<i> expose a fatal vulnerability.</i>

00:15:46.478 --> 00:15:51.116 align:start size:88% position:13%
<i> The Royal Navy is playing
 with fire literally.</i>

00:15:51.149 --> 00:15:57.122 align:start size:94% position:6%
JURENS: They could be ignited
if something went wrong
inside the turret itself.

00:15:57.155 --> 00:16:01.059 align:start size:94% position:6%
<i> NARRATOR: The drained wreck,
 shows the force of the blast
 goes through the ship</i>

00:16:01.092 --> 00:16:04.396
<i> from top to bottom.</i>

00:16:04.429 --> 00:16:08.200 align:start size:81% position:19%
<i> There's only one
 possible explanation.</i>

00:16:08.233 --> 00:16:13.105
<i> The blast doors are left open.</i>

00:16:13.138 --> 00:16:15.074
<i> This solves the mystery.</i>

00:16:16.808 --> 00:16:22.314 align:start size:88% position:13%
<i> A German shell hits
 the gun turret amidships
 and ignites the cordite.</i>

00:16:22.347 --> 00:16:30.055 align:start size:94% position:6%
<i> Fire spreads in seconds
