 main ammunition store below.</i>

00:16:30.088 --> 00:16:38.530
<i> The result - Invincible is
 torn in half, and the two
 gun turrets blown sky high.</i>

00:16:38.563 --> 00:16:43.702
JURENS: The impact of a
hundred tons of cordite
going off is enormous.

00:16:43.735 --> 00:16:48.173
It'll tear a ship in half,
it tore invincible in two.

00:16:48.206 --> 00:16:53.445
<i> NARRATOR: But why would
 Invincible's crew risk leaving
 the fire proof blast doors wide
 open?</i>

00:16:56.748 --> 00:17:00.285 align:start size:91% position:9%
DR. DELGADO: In naval
warfare there are different
approaches to fighting.

00:17:00.318 --> 00:17:07.125 line:0%
For the British it's rapidity
of fire, it's getting as many
shells off as fast as you can.

00:17:07.158 --> 00:17:12.197 align:start size:91% position:9%
PROF. GROVE: To increase
the rate of fire, magazine
doors are left open.

00:17:12.230 --> 00:17:15.300
The propellants are piled up.

00:17:15.333 --> 00:17:21.106 align:start size:97% position:3%
You hit anywhere in that
system, and you have a trail of
destruction going down

00:17:21.139 --> 00:17:22.741
into the magazines.

00:17:22.774 --> 00:17:25.177 align:start size:88% position:13%
Some magazine doors were
actually taken off.

00:17:25.210 --> 00:17:27.879
This is suicidal.

00:17:27.912 --> 00:17:34.619 align:start size:97% position:3%
<i> NARRATOR: This tactical error
 causes the Royal Navy to lose
 four of its best ships in less
 than three hours.</i>

00:17:34.652 --> 00:17:38.323
<i> And the battle is yet
 to reach its climax.</i>

00:17:40.391 --> 00:17:44.596
<i> Emptying the North Sea reveals
 the extent of the battlefield.</i>

00:17:44.629 --> 00:17:51.203
<i> This extraordinary drained
 landscape helps decipher
 the course of the action.</i>

00:17:51.236 --> 00:17:55.440
DR. DELGADO: When you look at
the entire battlefield, if you
were to take all the water away

00:17:55.473 --> 00:17:58.543 align:start size:94% position:6%
what you see is each warrior
where they fell.

00:17:58.576 --> 00:18:03.248
The position in which
they lie, the type of
damage that they took.

00:18:03.281 --> 00:18:11.156 align:start size:91% position:9%
In a larger forensic
sense, helps you re-create
the Battle of Jutland.

00:18:11.189 --> 00:18:16.161
<i> NARRATOR: About four hours into
 the battle, the Germans still
 believe they are facing a small</i>

00:18:16.194 --> 00:18:18.530
<i> part of the British fleet.</i>

00:18:18.563 --> 00:18:21.433
<i> Until the sea fog lifts.</i>

00:18:21.466 --> 00:18:26.705 align:start position:0%
PROF. GROVE: Suddenly Admiral
Scheer the German commander sees
the whole northern horizon

00:18:26.738 --> 00:18:30.709 align:start size:94% position:6%
light up with the gunfire of
a long line of British
battleships.

00:18:30.742 --> 00:18:33.578
This is his worst nightmare.

00:18:33.611 --> 00:18:40.185
<i> NARRATOR: Scheer is staring
 down the awesome firepower of
 the main British battle fleet.</i>

00:18:40.218 --> 00:18:46.658 align:start size:97% position:3%
<i> With fifty more ships than his
 enemy, Admiral Jellicoe has an
 extraordinary opportunity.</i>

00:18:46.691 --> 00:18:51.697 align:start position:0%
JELLICOE: The British didn't
just want victory they wanted
total annihilation of the German
fleet.

00:19:03.441 --> 00:19:07.379 align:start size:88% position:13%
PROF. GROVE: The classic
naval tactic is to cross
the enemy's T.

00:19:07.412 --> 00:19:12.184 align:start size:94% position:6%
Have your fleet sail across
the front of the enemy fleet,
so that you concentrate

00:19:12.217 --> 00:19:17.222 align:start size:91% position:9%
your firepower and the
enemy doesn't have as many
guns brought to bear.

00:19:17.255 --> 00:19:20.192 align:start size:91% position:9%
JELLICOE: It's a moment of
potential annihilation.

00:19:20.225 --> 00:19:24.763
This is the critical point
of the battle in many ways.

00:19:24.796 --> 00:19:32.003
<i> NARRATOR: By Crossing the
 T, the British bring the full
 force of their weapons to bear.</i>

00:19:32.036 --> 00:19:34.272
<i> They open fire.</i>

00:19:34.305 --> 00:19:38.210 align:start size:78% position:22%
<i> The German reaction
 is astonishing.</i>

00:19:38.243 --> 00:19:44.416
<i> They launch a daring escape
 bid, under heavy fire, risking
 collisions between their ships.</i>

00:19:44.449 --> 00:19:50.288
DR. DELGADO: As the British
cross the T, the Germans
execute an incredible feat

00:19:50.321 --> 00:19:53.558
of seamanship they turn 180.

00:19:53.591 --> 00:20:00.399 align:start size:94% position:6%
The British aren't even
expecting this and so the
Germans get out of the trap.

00:20:06.471 --> 00:20:10.809 align:start size:91% position:9%
JELLICOE: One moment
Jellicoe has all the German
fleet in his sights.

00:20:10.842 --> 00:20:12.811
Four minutes later nothing.

00:20:16.414 --> 00:20:24.356 align:start size:97% position:3%
<i> The Royal navy misses a golden
 opportunity to destroy the
 German fleet and change the
 course of the war.</i>

00:20:25.657 --> 00:20:29.594 align:start size:81% position:19%
<i> Draining the whole
 Jutland battlefield
 uncovers more wrecks,</i>

00:20:29.627 --> 00:20:34.866
<i> revealing how the Germans
 try to get away, and the
 British try to catch them.</i>

00:20:36.100 --> 00:20:41.840
<i> VINA heads south, hunting for
 the remains of a warship that
 reveals the lengths the Germans</i>

00:20:41.873 --> 00:20:46.278 align:start size:81% position:19%
<i> are willing to go to
 avoid capture</i>

00:20:46.311 --> 00:20:48.713
<i> Lutzow.</i>

00:20:48.746 --> 00:20:51.716 align:start size:84% position:16%
PROF. GROVE: Lutzow
was the newest of the
German battle cruisers,

00:20:51.749 --> 00:20:55.086
only just been commissioned
and she was a very fine ship.

00:20:55.119 --> 00:20:58.957
She had 8 12 inch guns,
very heavily protected.

00:21:04.429 --> 00:21:07.266 align:start size:91% position:9%
<i> NARRATOR: The waters of the
 North Sea drain away</i>

00:21:10.868 --> 00:21:18.109
<i> and Lutzow appears for the firs
 time in over a hundred years.</i>

00:21:18.142 --> 00:21:24.883 align:start size:91% position:9%
<i> This reveals astonishing
 new evidence of the
 ship's extraordinary fate.</i>

00:21:24.916 --> 00:21:27.385
<i> Lutzow lies face down.</i>

00:21:27.418 --> 00:21:34.959 align:start size:97% position:3%
<i> There's damage to the hull but
 unlike Invincible the ship
 hasn't been blown in half.</i>

00:21:34.992 --> 00:21:38.664
<i> So what sinks Lutzow?</i>

00:21:45.703 --> 00:21:52.410 align:start position:0%
<i> Draining away the cold dark
 waters of the North Sea, reveal
 one of the most powerful German</i>

00:21:52.443 --> 00:21:58.450
<i> warships at Jutland Lutzow.</i>

00:21:58.483 --> 00:22:03.355 align:start size:97% position:3% line:0%
DR. DELGADO: Lützow was heavily
mauled in the Battle, so much
so that it's almost

00:22:03.388 --> 00:22:08.460 align:start size:94% position:6% line:0%
like target practice for the
Royal Navy.

00:22:08.493 --> 00:22:13.565 align:start size:88% position:13%
<i> NARRATOR: Lutzow is
 heavily damaged, but the
 wreck reveals a surprise.</i>

00:22:14.332 --> 00:22:21.072
<i> The hull is not blown
 into separate pieces.</i>

00:22:21.105 --> 00:22:24.008
JELLICOE: It actually looks
like quite a complete hull.

00:22:24.041 --> 00:22:26.378 align:start size:94% position:6%
So not battle damage?
SURVEYOR: Not battle damage.

00:22:28.079 --> 00:22:34.452
<i> NARRATOR: Lutzow's intact hull
 is evidence it doesn't blow up,
 and Vina's cameras now suggest</i>

00:22:34.485 --> 00:22:37.055
<i> a possible reason why.</i>

00:22:37.088 --> 00:22:43.528
<i> The ship's highly explosive
 cordite is stored in these
 protective metal canisters.</i>

00:22:43.561 --> 00:22:48.900 align:start size:97% position:3%
JURENS: The German systems used
brass cartridge cases to carry
the propellant

00:22:48.933 --> 00:22:56.474 align:start size:94% position:6% line:0%
from place to place so it
was enclosed and less likely
to ignite.

00:22:56.507 --> 00:23:03.548 align:start size:97% position:3%
<i> NARRATOR: The drained seabed
 also shows the wreck lies
 nowhere near where the British
 fleet is operating.</i>

00:23:03.581 --> 00:23:10.488 align:start size:84% position:16%
<i> Suggesting it isn't
 sunk by the Royal Navy.</i>

00:23:10.521 --> 00:23:15.527
<i> The wreck reveals extensive
 damage below the waterline.</i>

00:23:15.560 --> 00:23:24.035 align:start size:97% position:3%
<i> Evidence that Lutzow is struck
 by weapons feared by all the
 ultimate battle ships</i>

00:23:24.068 --> 00:23:26.237
<i> Torpedoes.</i>

00:23:26.270 --> 00:23:34.479
<i> Unleashed not by the Royal
 Navy but by the Germans.</i>

00:23:34.512 --> 00:23:39.017 align:start size:88% position:13%
DR. DELGADO: Lutzow gets
away from the British
and Limping home,

00:23:39.050 --> 00:23:42.487
the crew realizes that the
ship's not going to make it.

00:23:42.520 --> 00:23:47.125 align:start size:91% position:9%
Rather than leave it afloat
for the British to capture
and make a trophy of,

00:23:47.158 --> 00:23:48.993
they decide to sink it.

00:23:49.026 --> 00:23:55.900
(explosion)

00:23:55.933 --> 00:24:03.141
<i> NARRATOR: Packed with advanced
 military technology, it's vital
 Lutzow doesn't fall into enemy
 hands.</i>

00:24:03.174 --> 00:24:05.210
<i> But that's not all.</i>

00:24:05.243 --> 00:24:10.548 align:start size:97% position:3%
DR. DELGADO: There's honour at
stake here and so they scuttle
it and they see nothing wrong
with that

00:24:10.581 --> 00:24:14.285 align:start size:97% position:3%
in fact it's the type of action
that will get you a medal.

00:24:14.318 --> 00:24:18.890 align:start size:94% position:6%
<i> NARRATOR: But the pride that
 drives the Germans to sink
 one of their best ships,</i>

00:24:18.923 --> 00:24:21.826
<i> comes at a terrible price.</i>

00:24:21.859 --> 00:24:24.596 line:0%
JELLICOE: There were men
trapped in the dynamo room.

00:24:24.629 --> 00:24:29.834 align:start size:97% position:3%
They had stayed on board to
keep the light running so that
their comrades could get off.

00:24:29.867 --> 00:24:33.538
They were the ones who were
told they're not going to get
off, the ship is going to be

00:24:33.571 --> 00:24:37.876
torpedoed by their
own torpedo boats.

00:24:37.909 --> 00:24:40.712
They knew they were gonna die.

00:24:48.653 --> 00:24:52.156
<i> NARRATOR: Eight hours into
 the battle night has fallen.</i>

00:24:52.189 --> 00:24:57.729 align:start size:88% position:13%
<i> The battered German
 fleet is running south to
 its base at Wilhemshaven.</i>

00:24:57.762 --> 00:25:05.069 align:start position:0%
<i> Draining the wrecks lying close
 to Germany reveals what happens
 when the British catch up to th
 Germans.</i>

00:25:05.102 --> 00:25:09.140
<i> Will they prevent their
 escape, destroy their
 fleet, and win the battle?</i>

00:25:13.578 --> 00:25:19.584
<i> VINA is following the course th
 British take as they pursue the
 Germans south in the darkness.</i>

00:25:19.617 --> 00:25:22.654
<i> Scans pick up another wreck.</i>

00:25:22.687 --> 00:25:29.727 align:start position:0%
<i> It's a British armoured cruiser
 a type of warship completely
 outgunned by German battleships</i>

00:25:29.760 --> 00:25:36.668 align:start size:94% position:6%
JELLICOE: We can see the
propeller shaft here and some
extensive damage in the stern
section.

00:25:38.169 --> 00:25:43.108
<i> NARRATOR: VINA has found the
 wreck of HMS Black Prince.</i>

00:25:52.216 --> 00:25:59.724 align:start size:91% position:9%
<i> Draining away the sea
 water reveals that the hull
 is broken clean in two.</i>

00:25:59.757 --> 00:26:05.296
<i> A gun turret lies exactly in th
 position where the hull splits.</i>

00:26:05.329 --> 00:26:11.636
<i> It's taken a direct hit and
 the magazines have blown up.</i>

00:26:13.671 --> 00:26:18.109
<i> From the drained wreck
 we can start to piece
 together what happens.</i>

00:26:21.846 --> 00:26:27.619 align:start position:0%
<i> While looking to re-join the
 main British fleet, Black Princ
 runs into a formation of twelve</i>

00:26:27.652 --> 00:26:35.160
<i> German battle ships and
 in the darkness mistakes
 them for the Royal Navy.</i>

00:26:38.729 --> 00:26:46.271
<i> The wreck lies pointing to the
 North East, confirming Black
 Prince is trying desperately to
 steer away from the Germans.</i>

00:26:47.338 --> 00:26:52.710 line:0%
PROF. GROVE: We know from
the archaeology now that the
ship appears to have turned,

00:26:52.743 --> 00:26:54.779 align:start size:81% position:19% line:0%
so they knew what
danger they were in.

00:26:54.812 --> 00:26:56.714 align:start size:88% position:13%
They didn't, they didn't
just sail into it.

00:26:56.747 --> 00:27:00.284
But they turned too
little, too late.

00:27:00.317 --> 00:27:05.123
(explosions)

00:27:05.156 --> 00:27:10.161 align:start size:84% position:16%
<i> NARRATOR: All eight
 hundred and fifty seven
 men on board are lost.</i>

00:27:14.131 --> 00:27:19.070
<i> Black Prince is not the
 only ship the British lose
 as they pursue the enemy.</i>

00:27:19.103 --> 00:27:22.073
<i> At least four more
 warships are sunk.</i>

00:27:23.374 --> 00:27:29.914 align:start size:94% position:6%
<i> The German rear guard action
 uses torpedo attacks to keep
 Jellicoe's force at bay.</i>

00:27:29.947 --> 00:27:34.419
<i> Knowing his fleet isn't
 well equipped for night
 fighting he disengages.</i>

00:27:38.789 --> 00:27:44.128
<i> The following day the Germans
 make it safely back to base.</i>

00:27:44.161 --> 00:27:50.968 align:start size:88% position:13%
<i> The Royal Navy fails
 to stop their escape,
 after just twelve hours,</i>

00:27:51.001 --> 00:27:53.738
<i> the battle of Jutland is over.</i>

00:27:56.507 --> 00:28:01.913
<i> Ten thousand sailors are dead.</i>

00:28:01.946 --> 00:28:09.087 align:start size:94% position:6%
<i> As dawn breaks over the North
 Sea, bodies from each side,
 start to wash up along the
 Danish Coast.</i>

00:28:11.822 --> 00:28:19.497 align:start position:0%
<i> Even though they have retreated
 the Germans claim victory
 because they have fewer losses.</i>

00:28:19.530 --> 00:28:23.101 align:start size:81% position:19%
<i> The British have
 lost fourteen ships.</i>

00:28:23.134 --> 00:28:27.538 align:start size:84% position:16%
<i> Admiral Scheer's German
 fleet, just eleven.</i>

00:28:27.571 --> 00:28:30.742 align:start size:88% position:13%
JAMES DELGADO: He has
inflicted incredible
losses on the Royal Navy,

00:28:30.775 --> 00:28:36.380 align:start size:97% position:3%
he has sunk more British
tonnage and killed more British
sailors than he has lost,

00:28:36.413 --> 00:28:40.719 align:start size:91% position:9%
so to his mind
and for the Kaiser and for
Germany this is a victory.

00:28:45.222 --> 00:28:50.862 align:start position:0%
<i> NARRATOR: But despite losing
 more ships and men, Admiral
 Jellicoe's fleet ultimately win</i>

00:28:50.895 --> 00:28:56.367 align:start size:88% position:13%
<i> the battle because
 they now have complete
 command of the North Sea.</i>

00:28:56.400 --> 00:29:03.908 align:start position:0%
<i> For the rest of the war, German
 battleships never dare challeng
 the full might of the Royal Nav
 again.</i>

00:29:03.941 --> 00:29:08.112 align:start size:94% position:6%
PROF. GROVE: The crucial
strategic fact is that the
British Grand Fleet is acting

00:29:08.145 --> 00:29:12.817 align:start size:91% position:9%
as the stopper in
the bottle, to stop German
surface ships getting out.

00:29:12.850 --> 00:29:18.790 align:start size:94% position:6%
<i> NARRATOR: Taking control of
 the sea after this battle is
 decisive in winning World War
 One.</i>

00:29:18.823 --> 00:29:23.361
<i> For two years after Jutland,
 Britain blockades German ports.</i>

00:29:23.394 --> 00:29:28.366 align:start size:88% position:13%
<i> Slowly starving the
 country into submission.</i>

00:29:28.399 --> 00:29:35.206
<i> In 1918 Britain and her
 allies are victorious.</i>

00:29:35.239 --> 00:29:41.979 align:start size:88% position:13%
<i> Germany is forced to
 give up its navy without
 another shot being fired.</i>

00:29:42.012 --> 00:29:50.054
<i> But if the conflict is over, wh
 does the entire German fleet no
 lie on the bottom of the sea?</i>

00:29:54.992 --> 00:29:58.896 line:0%
<i> (music)</i>

00:29:58.929 --> 00:30:03.835 align:start size:88% position:13% line:0%
<i> When World War One ends
 on November the eleventh
 nineteen eighteen,</i>

00:30:03.868 --> 00:30:10.208 align:start size:94% position:6%
<i> the German high seas fleet is
 anchored in its main base.</i>

00:30:10.241 --> 00:30:17.615
<i> But a little over six months
 later, it's on the sea bed
 five hundred miles away here,</i>

00:30:17.648 --> 00:30:19.851
<i> off the northern
 tip of Scotland.</i>

00:30:23.554 --> 00:30:30.562
<i> The Orkney Islands cluster
 around a gigantic natural
 harbour called Scapa Flow.</i>

00:30:36.033 --> 00:30:40.037 align:start size:94% position:6%
<i> Using the latest data from
 detailed underwater scanning,</i>

00:30:40.070 --> 00:30:43.374
<i> it's possible to drain
 away the waters here.</i>

00:30:44.875 --> 00:30:49.914
<i> The sea pushes back to
 expose narrow passages
 between the islands.</i>

00:30:53.951 --> 00:31:02.026 align:start position:0%
<i> This ancient submerged landscap
 makes Scapa Flow one of the
 largest natural deep water
 harbours in the world.</i>

00:31:05.696 --> 00:31:08.867 align:start size:88% position:13%
<i> The perfect place to
 imprison a battle fleet.</i>

00:31:16.173 --> 00:31:20.945
<i> Skipper John Thornton knows
 these waters better than most.</i>

00:31:22.012 --> 00:31:27.285 align:start size:97% position:3% line:0%
THORNTON: Scapa's a very
special and unique place, in
that the waters are sheltered,

00:31:27.318 --> 00:31:30.087 align:start size:91% position:9% line:0%
it's the reason why it was
used as a British naval
harbour.

00:31:30.120 --> 00:31:33.224 align:start position:0%
Also its position geographically
is very strategic.

00:31:38.762 --> 00:31:46.404 align:start position:0%
<i> NARRATOR: November 1918 the
 defeated German fleet is ordere
 to set sail into captivity.</i>

00:31:47.138 --> 00:31:53.744 align:start size:94% position:6% line:0%
DR. DELGADO: The Germans
with flags flying steam out,
and come into Scapa Flow,

00:31:53.777 --> 00:31:57.748 line:0%
and there they're
to be interned.

00:31:57.781 --> 00:32:02.954 align:start position:0%
<i> NARRATOR: They are anchored at
 Scapa while politicians from th
 Victorious allies decide</i>

00:32:02.987 --> 00:32:08.059
<i> how they'll divide up these
 spoils of war worth billions.</i>

00:32:08.092 --> 00:32:10.294
PROF. GROVE: It must
have given the Germans
terrible feelings of,

00:32:10.327 --> 00:32:13.264 align:start size:91% position:9%
of disgrace and humiliation
and a determination

00:32:13.297 --> 00:32:18.669 align:start size:97% position:3% line:0%
not to allow these ships to
fall into the hands of Allies.

00:32:18.702 --> 00:32:22.406 align:start size:91% position:9%
<i> NARRATOR: The political
 wrangling over which of the
 allies will get which</i>

00:32:22.439 --> 00:32:26.077
<i> of the German ships continues.</i>

00:32:26.110 --> 00:32:27.845
<i> June 1919.</i>

00:32:27.879 --> 00:32:33.150 align:start position:0%
<i> Nearly Seven months after their
 capture, the seventy four Germa
 ships remain at anchor</i>

00:32:33.183 --> 00:32:35.619
<i> under Royal Navy Guard.</i>

00:32:35.652 --> 00:32:40.057 align:start size:97% position:3%
<i> Their skeleton crews prisoners
 on their own vessels.</i>

00:32:40.090 --> 00:32:45.530 align:start size:97% position:3%
THORNTON: They had to be fed
and supplied from Germany the
British refused to supply them
locally.

00:32:47.564 --> 00:32:50.735
They're on the ships with no
entertainment, no heating.

00:32:50.768 --> 00:32:54.072 align:start size:91% position:9%
So these poor people would
have had a grim time.

00:32:57.174 --> 00:33:03.114
<i> NARRATOR: Using the latest
 sonar data, draining Scapa Flow
 reveals the fate of the German
 fleet.</i>

00:33:08.719 --> 00:33:17.261 align:start size:97% position:3%
<i> As the water recedes, it
 uncovers the wreck of one of
 the most advanced ships in the
 entire German navy.</i>

00:33:17.294 --> 00:33:20.798
<i> Packing no fewer than
 ten twelve inch guns.</i>

00:33:20.831 --> 00:33:28.139
<i> Markgraf is brand new when
 the war starts in 1914.</i>

00:33:28.172 --> 00:33:34.412
<i> The drained wreck now reveals
 huge chunks cut out of the hull
 more recently by salvagers,</i>

00:33:34.445 --> 00:33:37.681 align:start size:84% position:16%
<i> searching for
 valuable scrap metals.</i>

00:33:37.714 --> 00:33:42.086
<i> The ship capsizes
 as it goes down.</i>

00:33:42.119 --> 00:33:49.527
<i> There are no signs of battle
 damage along the giant hull
 So what sinks the Markgraf?</i>

00:33:49.560 --> 00:33:54.331 align:start size:88% position:13%
<i> These portholes are
 clearly visible close to
 what was the waterline.</i>

00:33:54.364 --> 00:33:57.535
<i> And they appear to be open.</i>

00:34:04.241 --> 00:34:08.679 align:start size:88% position:13%
<i> From the Dive Boat
 Karin, a team sets out
 to investigate the wreck.</i>

00:34:08.712 --> 00:34:18.722
<i> (music)</i>

00:34:18.755 --> 00:34:24.161
<i> (music)</i>

00:34:24.194 --> 00:34:29.267 align:start size:94% position:6%
<i> They want to find out if the
 portholes were opened before
 or after the ship sinks.</i>

00:34:35.772 --> 00:34:40.478 align:start size:88% position:13% line:0%
STEVENSON: It's
a huge ship, your only
impression is just scale.

00:34:40.511 --> 00:34:42.580
Minute after minute just
slowly moving along it.

00:34:42.613 --> 00:34:47.751
It just keeps going and keeps
going, she's a massive ship.

00:34:47.784 --> 00:34:52.189
<i> NARRATOR: Dan's picture clearly
 shows an open porthole.</i>

00:34:52.222 --> 00:34:55.426
STEVENSON: Here's one
with the actual glass
portal still in place.

00:34:55.459 --> 00:34:58.229 align:start size:91% position:9%
JELLICOE: But it's open
STEVENSON: It's wide open.

00:34:58.262 --> 00:35:04.235 align:start size:97% position:3%
There was no way a diver could
have got on the inside to have
unbolted that porthole so

00:35:04.268 --> 00:35:09.373 align:start size:94% position:6%
it had to have been sunk with
the with the port hole wide
open.

00:35:09.406 --> 00:35:14.845
<i> NARRATOR: It's a vital clue
 revealing an amazing event.</i>

00:35:14.878 --> 00:35:17.748
DR. DELGADO: One of the
most incredible acts in
the history of any Navy.

00:35:22.186 --> 00:35:28.159 align:start size:91% position:9%
<i> NARRATOR: There are more
 German Battleships lying on
 the bottom of Scapa Flow.</i>

00:35:28.192 --> 00:35:36.500
<i> These wrecks are undamaged
 no sign of explosions, no
 holes made by torpedoes.</i>

00:35:36.533 --> 00:35:39.537
<i> It can only mean one thing.</i>

00:35:40.938 --> 00:35:47.411 align:start position:0%
DR. DELGADO: What the Germans do
under the noses of their captors
is to simultaneously scuttle

00:35:47.444 --> 00:35:50.148 align:start size:84% position:16%
their fleet that is
send it to the bottom.

00:35:52.583 --> 00:35:57.188 align:start size:91% position:9%
<i> NARRATOR: Shocked witnesses
 take pictures of the ships
 actually sinking.</i>

00:36:02.426 --> 00:36:05.763
<i> It's June twenty first, 1919.</i>

00:36:05.796 --> 00:36:12.403 align:start size:94% position:6%
<i> The decision on how the
 German fleet is to be divided
 up is due to be announced.</i>

00:36:12.436 --> 00:36:17.408 align:start size:91% position:9%
<i> Under secret orders from
 Germany, Fleet commander
 Von Reuter has been quietly</i>

00:36:17.441 --> 00:36:22.012 align:start size:78% position:22%
<i> preparing this
 moment for months.</i>

00:36:22.045 --> 00:36:26.250 align:start size:97% position:3%
PROF. GROVE: The Germans saw
scuttling the fleet as actually
a way at getting back at their
captors.

00:36:26.283 --> 00:36:28.419
It was actually an
aggressive move.

00:36:28.452 --> 00:36:33.958 align:start size:94% position:6%
The Germans had got their
revenge at last, three years
after the battle of Jutland.

00:36:33.991 --> 00:36:39.463
<i> NARRATOR: Sinking their entire
 fleet, prevents the allies from
 getting their hands on the
 German ships.</i>

00:36:39.496 --> 00:36:45.336
<i> (music)</i>

00:36:45.369 --> 00:36:53.978
<i> But sinking seventy four
 warships at the same time,
 requires more than just
 leaving portholes open.</i>

00:36:54.011 --> 00:36:59.717
<i> How did they do it?</i>

00:36:59.750 --> 00:37:03.954 align:start position:0%
<i> David Manley is a naval
 architect with an unusual skill</i>

00:37:03.987 --> 00:37:08.859
<i> His job is testing ships
 by trying to sink them.</i>

00:37:08.892 --> 00:37:11.929
<i> HMS Belfast is a
 typical warship.</i>

00:37:11.962 --> 00:37:17.368 align:start size:88% position:13%
<i> It has weak spots if
 you know what to look for
 and have the right tools.</i>

00:37:17.401 --> 00:37:22.039
MANLEY: We're well below the
waterline here, and this pipe
is one of the ways in which you

00:37:22.072 --> 00:37:29.346 line:0%
would very easily uh sink a ship
of this size and all you need is
just a sledgehammer like this.

00:37:29.379 --> 00:37:33.417
Hit the pipe hard, give it a
good whack, split it apart.

00:37:33.450 --> 00:37:35.919
The force of the water coming
out of here well I mean if,

00:37:35.952 --> 00:37:37.955
if you were standing near it,
it would take your head off.

00:37:37.988 --> 00:37:41.759 align:start size:84% position:16%
It would enough to
fill this compartment
in just a few minutes.

00:37:41.792 --> 00:37:43.794
And then everybody is just
heading for the upper deck.

00:37:47.364 --> 00:37:54.071 align:start position:0%
<i> NARRATOR: With their ships
 flooding, the German crews have
 to evacuate from deep within th
 hull.</i>

00:37:54.104 --> 00:37:57.408 align:start position:0%
MANLEY: The amount of
coordination, is just fantastic.

00:37:57.441 --> 00:38:03.347 align:start size:97% position:3%
To do that under the eyes of
the British who are guarding
the fleet is a feat of amazing
skill, amazing ingenuity.

00:38:12.422 --> 00:38:14.391 align:start size:91% position:9%
Right so it's taken
me a few minutes to get up
here from the engine room,

00:38:14.424 --> 00:38:17.361 align:start position:0%
but at least I'm on the upper
deck, I'm safe and I can escape.

00:38:21.131 --> 00:38:26.036 align:start position:0%
<i> NARRATOR: But in Scapa Flow whe
 the Germans reach the decks of
 their sinking ships</i>

00:38:26.069 --> 00:38:29.440
<i> they are far from safe.</i>

00:38:29.473 --> 00:38:36.080 align:start size:97% position:3%
<i> This cemetery bears witness to
 the tragic consequences when
 British guards realize what is
 happening.</i>

00:38:42.486 --> 00:38:47.825 align:start size:97% position:3%
JELLICOE: Here is the last
resting place of the nine or so
German sailors who were killed

00:38:47.858 --> 00:38:49.727
during the scuttle.

00:38:49.760 --> 00:38:53.030 align:start size:97% position:3%
Walter Schumann, was the
commander of the SMS Markgraf.

00:38:53.063 --> 00:38:58.469
He was shot after
he had surrendered.

00:38:58.502 --> 00:39:04.842
They were effectively
the last casualties of
the First World War.

00:39:04.875 --> 00:39:08.579 align:start size:91% position:9%
<i> NARRATOR: German sailors
 make the supreme sacrifice.</i>

00:39:08.612 --> 00:39:13.817 align:start size:84% position:16%
<i> Shot while surrendering
 after preventing the
 capture of their ships.</i>

00:39:13.850 --> 00:39:17.755
<i> (music)</i>

00:39:17.788 --> 00:39:24.194 align:start size:84% position:16%
<i> In 1919 Seventy four
 German warships are
 scuttled in Scapa Flow.</i>

00:39:24.227 --> 00:39:30.601
<i> But draining the water reveals
 only seven actual shipwrecks.</i>

00:39:30.634 --> 00:39:39.009 align:start position:0%
<i> And ghostly imprints left behin
 by the others, what happens to
 the missing warships?</i>

00:39:42.512 --> 00:39:46.016 line:0%
<i> (music)</i>

00:39:46.049 --> 00:39:50.421 align:start size:94% position:6% line:0%
<i> As a boy Ian Murray Taylor
 lived on the shores of Scapa.</i>

00:39:50.454 --> 00:39:56.093
<i> He's the last man alive
 who went inside one of the
 scuttled German warships.</i>

00:39:56.126 --> 00:40:01.465 align:start size:94% position:6%
TAYLOR: I went right down
through the ship, it was very
duh dirty and dusty

00:40:01.498 --> 00:40:06.737 align:start size:94% position:6% line:0%
and the air was a bit whiffy
and you could look down on to
the seabed below.

00:40:08.338 --> 00:40:16.079 align:start size:94% position:6%
<i> NARRATOR: In 1924 Ian's
 grandfather Thomas MacKenzie
 starts recovering the sunken
 wrecks.</i>

00:40:16.112 --> 00:40:20.884 align:start size:97% position:3%
<i> His family films the
 extraordinary work that brings
 them back to surface</i>

00:40:20.917 --> 00:40:25.055 align:start size:88% position:13%
<i> so the valuable steel can
 be sold for scrap.</i>

00:40:25.088 --> 00:40:28.091
<i> Divers seal holes in the hull.</i>

00:40:28.124 --> 00:40:34.932
<i> Compressed air is pumped in
 until the ship is buoyant like
 filling a balloon with air.</i>

00:40:34.965 --> 00:40:39.570
<i> Then the massive battle ships
 explode back to the surface.</i>

00:40:43.106 --> 00:40:50.914 align:start size:94% position:6%
<i> In this way, during the 1920s
 and 30s all but seven of the
 German wrecks are raised.</i>

00:40:50.947 --> 00:40:55.719
<i> Their hulls are towed away
 and broken into pieces.</i>

00:40:55.752 --> 00:40:59.223
<i> There are willing buyers
 for the salvaged metal.</i>

00:40:59.256 --> 00:41:04.761
<i> Among them, Adolf Hitler. He's
 building a powerful new navy.</i>

00:41:04.794 --> 00:41:11.668
<i> Scrap metal from The German
 fleet returns home to be melted
 down and re-used for a new</i>

00:41:11.701 --> 00:41:14.505
<i> and deadly purpose.</i>

00:41:14.538 --> 00:41:20.611
DR. DELGADO: What also happened
in the aftermath of Scapa Flow
is for the Germans in particular

00:41:20.644 --> 00:41:23.714
a sense of a War that
really hadn't ended.

00:41:23.747 --> 00:41:29.119 line:0%
What followed was a grim
determination to come back
and fight that War again.

00:41:31.821 --> 00:41:38.762 align:start position:0%
<i> NARRATOR: 1939 The Nazis use th
 steel from the sunken German
 megaships to prepare</i>

00:41:38.795 --> 00:41:43.300
<i> a new generation of weapons.</i>

00:41:43.333 --> 00:41:45.669 align:start size:97% position:3%
PROF. GROVE: There is something
of a full circle here.

00:41:45.702 --> 00:41:48.205 align:start size:84% position:16%
The Germans are getting
their own back

00:41:48.238 --> 00:41:51.241 align:start size:94% position:6%
<i> NARRATOR: They've learned the
 bitter lessons of Jutland.</i>

00:41:51.274 --> 00:41:56.780 align:start size:88% position:13%
<i> This time they'll
 use submarines, not
 battleships to wage war.</i>

00:41:56.813 --> 00:42:05.589 align:start size:94% position:6% line:0%
PROF. EPKENHANS: The era
of the big battleships which
dominated the seas, was over.

00:42:05.622 --> 00:42:12.629
<i> NARRATOR: In Scapa Flow the
 sinking of one ship marks the
 dramatic end of this era.</i>

00:42:12.662 --> 00:42:17.634
<i> Investigators scanning this
 unique wreck for the British
 Ministry of Defence reveal</i>

00:42:17.667 --> 00:42:22.139
<i> a capsized hull resting on
 its twisted superstructure.</i>

00:42:27.944 --> 00:42:34.051 align:start size:97% position:3%
<i> But only by draining the water
 away, is it possible to see
 close up the evidence</i>

00:42:34.084 --> 00:42:36.887
<i> of what sinks this
 veteran of Jutland?</i>

00:42:39.255 --> 00:42:41.125
<i> HMS Royal Oak.</i>

00:42:43.026 --> 00:42:48.865 align:start size:88% position:13%
<i> One side appears
 undamaged, but the other
 displays gaping wounds.</i>

00:42:48.898 --> 00:42:53.137
<i> What sends Royal Oak to the
 bottom in this safe haven?</i>

00:42:55.705 --> 00:42:59.376 align:start size:94% position:6% line:0%
LAWRENCE: The level of impact
and the destruction, uh

00:42:59.409 --> 00:43:01.612 align:start size:97% position:3% line:0%
it was just, just mind-bending
to see

00:43:04.681 --> 00:43:09.653
<i> NARRATOR: October 1939. One
 month into World War Two.</i>

00:43:09.686 --> 00:43:14.258
<i> The Nazis want revenge
 on the British and are
 hunting for targets.</i>

00:43:15.459 --> 00:43:22.399
PROF. EPKENHANS: It was of great
symbolic value to hit the Royal
Navy in its heart and Scapa Flow

00:43:22.432 --> 00:43:27.337 align:start size:84% position:16%
was of course the heart
of the Royal Navy.

00:43:27.370 --> 00:43:32.409
<i> NARRATOR: Royal Oak
 lies safely at anchor.</i>

00:43:32.442 --> 00:43:38.115
<i> The crew, including over a
 hundred and fifty young
 saylors, under eighteen</i>

00:43:38.148 --> 00:43:42.686
<i> suspect nothing.</i>

00:43:42.719 --> 00:43:49.326
<i> The guns are trained
 on the skies in case
 of Nazi air raids...</i>

00:43:49.359 --> 00:43:53.664
<i> But the real threat lies below.</i>

00:43:57.367 --> 00:44:03.674
<i> At the time of Jutland, old
 ships are deliberately sunk to
 block Scapa's narrow entrances</i>

00:44:03.707 --> 00:44:06.476
<i> and defend against
 submarine attacks.</i>

00:44:06.509 --> 00:44:11.682 align:start size:84% position:16%
<i> But now they're falling
 apart and it's possible
 to sneak past them.</i>

00:44:13.950 --> 00:44:17.220 align:start size:91% position:9% line:0%
PROF. GROVE: There
were now passages for
submarines to get through,

00:44:17.253 --> 00:44:21.992 align:start size:88% position:13% line:0%
where a daring submarine
commander could actually
get into the Flow.

00:44:26.996 --> 00:44:31.268
<i> NARRATOR: October
 14th 12:58 A.M.</i>

00:44:31.301 --> 00:44:38.876 align:start position:0%
<i> U-Boat Ace Gunther Prien
 stealthily navigates U47 throug
 the poorly defended channels of
 Scapa.</i>

00:44:42.779 --> 00:44:48.385
<i> The men and boys aboard
 Royal Oak have no idea of
 the danger they are in.</i>

00:44:49.152 --> 00:44:55.893
<i> Examining the drained wreck
 reveals Prien's first torpedo
 smashes clean through the bow.</i>

00:45:02.999 --> 00:45:07.471 align:start position:0%
LAWRENCE: They all thought that
it was an explosion in the paint
store or of the aviation

00:45:07.504 --> 00:45:14.311 align:start size:91% position:9%
fuel store um and uh they,
they didn't realise they
were being attacked.

00:45:14.344 --> 00:45:19.983
<i> NARRATOR: The wreck now
 reveals three more torpedo
 strikes along the hull.</i>

00:45:20.016 --> 00:45:25.822 align:start size:88% position:13%
<i> These are the deathblows,
 smashing huge holes in
 Royal Oak.</i>

00:45:25.855 --> 00:45:34.431
(explosions)

00:45:34.464 --> 00:45:37.434 align:start size:81% position:19%
<i> Fatally wounded
 below the waterline.</i>

00:45:37.467 --> 00:45:39.770
<i> Royal Oak begins to capsize.</i>

00:45:41.204 --> 00:45:48.512
<i> For most there's no escape.
 The ship sinks in minutes.</i>

00:45:48.545 --> 00:45:52.015
<i> 833 sailors are lost.</i>

00:45:54.017 --> 00:45:58.122
<i> More than a 130 of
 them are just boys.</i>

00:46:00.190 --> 00:46:03.860
PROF. EPKENHANS: It was
received with enormous
enthusiasm in Germany.

00:46:03.893 --> 00:46:09.432 align:start position:0%
Hitler sent his aeroplane to
Kiel to pick up the crew and fly
to Berlin where people hailed

00:46:09.465 --> 00:46:12.269
them and everybody was happy.

00:46:15.872 --> 00:46:20.243 align:start size:97% position:3%
DR. DELGADO: So it's one of the
greatest ironies perhaps in
naval warfare that out of the

00:46:20.276 --> 00:46:25.315
scuttling at Scapa Flow comes
the steel that helps re-arm
Germany and one of those ships,

00:46:25.348 --> 00:46:31.855 align:start size:91% position:9%
one of these U-Boats,
sneaks in to Scapa Flow and
sinks a veteran of Jutland.

00:46:31.888 --> 00:46:34.858
If that's not ironic
I don't know what is.

00:46:34.891 --> 00:46:39.896
<i> (music)</i>

00:46:39.929 --> 00:46:42.599 line:0%
<i> NARRATOR: The wrecks of
 Jutland and Scapa Flow</i>

00:46:42.632 --> 00:46:46.603 line:0%
<i> bear silent witness to a
 lost age of naval warfare.</i>

00:46:46.636 --> 00:46:53.877 line:0%
<i> (explosions)</i>

00:46:53.910 --> 00:47:02.552 line:0%
<i> But draining the Ultimate
 Battleships reveals the world
 changing impact of the day they
 fought to the death</i>

00:47:02.585 --> 00:47:09.359 line:0%
<i> and the courage of the men and
 boys who served aboard them.</i>

